  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date the Department of State recorded $4.8B in contract obligations across 16,476 awards with an average award size of $293K. Obligations fell 51.0% year over year. The agency’s fiscal-year history shows higher spending in prior years, including $11.9B in FY22, $11.7B in FY23, $11.7B in FY24, and $9.9B in FY25, with $9.5B in FY21. In the current fiscal year the largest categories of spending are Security Guards and Patrol Services ($789M), Other Computer Related Services ($585M), and Computer Systems Design Services ($523M). Top vendors so far are CGI FEDERAL INC. ($166M), INTER-CON SECURITY SYSTEMS, INC. ($148M), and TRIPLE CANOPY INC ($142M). The mix shows continued emphasis on security services and IT support alongside administrative, facilities, and banking services.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has State's contract spending changed recently?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 51.0% year over year. The agency spent $4.8B in FY2026 to date after peaking at $11.9B in FY22 and remaining near $11.7B in FY23 and FY24, then $9.9B in FY25. That sequence shows materially lower obligations in the current fiscal year compared with the previous several years."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are the main buys and who gets the money in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Security Guards and Patrol Services leads category spending at $789M, followed by Other Computer Related Services at $585M and Computer Systems Design Services at $523M. Vendors receiving the most obligations include CGI FEDERAL INC. ($166M), INTER-CON SECURITY SYSTEMS, INC. ($148M), and TRIPLE CANOPY INC ($142M). These categories and vendors indicate focus areas in security and IT support."
      }
    ]
  },
